## 4. The Priority Shift — "How do you handle a situation where a stakeholder changes a deadline or a priority mid-sprint?"

**SHORT:**
Legal at Ultra Mobile would routinely request copy and disclaimer changes mid-sprint — after getting caught enough times scrambling to push code for text updates, I made the legal content CMS-able so the team could accommodate last-minute changes without touching code or disrupting the sprint.

**STAR:**
- **Situation:** At Ultra Mobile the legal team regularly made changes to compliance copy, disclaimers, and terms mid-sprint. These weren't small requests — they were mandatory, couldn't be deferred, and every time it happened the team had to stop what they were doing, make the code change, go through review, and redeploy. It was a recurring sprint disruptor that nobody had solved because it felt like a people problem rather than a technical one.
- **Task:** Find a way to absorb legal's last-minute changes without derailing the development sprint every time they came in.
- **Action:** The obvious answer would have been to use WordPress's default content areas, but our pages had unique custom designs where legal disclaimers lived underneath specific sections — not in any standard location WordPress natively handled. I had to build custom ACF field groups tied to each page section so that the legal disclaimers underneath each unique design block were independently editable in the CMS. Legal could then go directly into WordPress, find the right section, and update their disclaimers without understanding the underlying page structure or filing an engineering ticket.
- **Result:** Mid-sprint disruptions from legal changes dropped significantly. The team reclaimed sprint capacity that had been quietly leaking to one-off copy changes, and legal had the autonomy to move at their own pace without waiting on an engineering queue. The broader lesson: when the same interruption keeps happening, it's not a people problem — it's a systems problem waiting to be solved.

