## 5. The Mentorship — "Tell me about a time you mentored a junior developer or helped a peer solve a complex problem."

**SHORT:**
As UX Developer at Ultra Mobile I introduced Storybook to the team — built out the initial component library, ran walkthroughs so junior developers could see components in isolation before touching the main app, and it became the shared reference that reduced UI regressions and shortened code review cycles.

**STAR:**
- **Situation:** The frontend team at Ultra Mobile was building UI components directly inside the app with no isolated development environment. Junior developers had no reliable way to see how a component should look and behave across its different states, which led to inconsistent implementations, visual regressions, and long code review cycles catching issues that should have been caught earlier.
- **Task:** Establish a component development standard that would raise the baseline for the whole team without adding friction to the existing workflow.
- **Action:** I introduced Storybook to the team — set it up, built out the initial component library with stories covering the key states for each shared component, and ran walkthroughs so junior developers understood how to develop and test components in isolation before integrating them into the app. I framed every code review from that point around "does this match the Storybook story" rather than just subjective feedback, which gave juniors a concrete reference instead of moving goalposts.
- **Result:** UI regressions dropped because developers could catch visual inconsistencies in Storybook before they ever hit the main app. Code review cycles shortened, and junior developers started writing their own stories as part of their normal workflow — the standard became self-sustaining without me having to enforce it.

