## 6. The Difficult Person — "Describe a time you had to work with someone who was difficult or uncooperative. How did you manage that relationship?"

**SHORT:**
At Ultra Mobile a contractor kept submitting PRs late in the sprint with solutions that needed refactoring — instead of raising it in standup or escalating, I reached out directly and offered to work through it together if he was stuck, and the pattern improved once he knew he had a resource.

**STAR:**
- **Situation:** At Ultra Mobile we had a contractor on the team who consistently submitted his PRs late in the sprint. Standup updates were always "working on it" with little visibility into progress, and when the PRs did come in they sometimes required significant refactoring — which created pressure on the rest of the team at the worst possible time in the sprint cycle.
- **Task:** Address the pattern without creating conflict or making him feel called out in front of the team.
- **Action:** I didn't bring it up in standup or go to the lead first. I reached out to him directly and kept it simple — told him I noticed he was deep in some of his tickets and asked if there was anything he was stuck on that we could work through together. I framed it as an offer, not a critique. What came out of that conversation was that he was hesitant to ask for help because he didn't want to look like he couldn't handle the work, so he'd keep grinding quietly instead of flagging early.
- **Result:** Once he knew he had a resource without judgment, he started reaching out earlier when he hit blockers. The late PRs became less frequent and the refactor requests dropped because we were catching issues in conversation before they made it into code. The lesson: "difficult" behavior usually has a reason behind it — and a direct, private, non-judgmental conversation gets to that reason faster than anything else.

