## 8. The Innovation — "Describe a time you introduced a new technology or process that improved the team's efficiency."

**SHORT:**
At Ultra Mobile I built the Promotions Manager — a self-serve tool that let marketing configure and launch promotional campaigns without engineering involvement — cutting promotion deployment from 3 sprints down to 1 and freeing the team to focus on platform work instead of one-off campaign builds.

**STAR:**
- **Situation:** Every new Mint Mobile promotion required engineering to hardcode campaign logic — discount types, eligibility rules, time windows — which meant each promotion took roughly 3 sprints to design, build, test, and ship. Marketing was constantly blocked waiting on engineering bandwidth.
- **Task:** Remove engineering from the critical path for routine promotion launches without sacrificing reliability on a platform serving millions of subscribers.
- **Action:** I built the Promotions Manager — a configurable rules engine with an admin UI that let marketing define and schedule promotions themselves. I designed it around the patterns I'd already seen repeat across campaigns: discount types, plan eligibility, stacking rules, and activation windows. During sprint planning we would review the Figma designs upfront and identify any visual elements the existing CMS fields couldn't cover — those gaps became the only custom development work scoped into that sprint. Everything the Promotions Manager already handled shipped without engineering touching it. Engineering's remaining role was a final validation pass ensuring the promotion message was consistent across the entire customer journey: homepage, plans page, product page, cart, and checkout, all matching the Figma designs before launch.
- **Result:** Promotion deployment dropped from 3 sprints to 1. Marketing could configure and launch campaigns independently, engineering was reduced to a quality gate rather than a bottleneck, and Ultra Mobile was no longer limited to the typical Fall/Black Friday window — they could launch promotions throughout the year without competing for engineering sprint capacity.

