## 9. The Pressure — "Tell me about the most high-pressure technical situation you've been in. How did you stay focused?"

**SHORT:**
During a release call at Ultra Mobile we discovered the CTA button on the plans page was routing to a 404 instead of the product page — my teammate was digging into it, so I opened a parallel track, traced the broken route, wrote a quick fix PR, and our lead used it to unblock the release on the spot.

**STAR:**
- **Situation:** We were on a live release call at Ultra Mobile when someone flagged that the CTA button on the plans page — the button that drives customers from plan selection into the product page — was hitting a 404. The whole team was on the line with a one-hour window to resolve it or pull the release entirely. For a ecomm platform where that button is one of the highest-traffic actions on the site, a 404 on release day was not an option.
- **Task:** Get the release unblocked within the window without crowding the teammate who was already on it.
- **Action:** I watched for a moment, saw my teammate was deep in the root cause investigation but hadn't landed on a fix yet. I opened a parallel track and started tracing the issue myself. The slug was being resolved by a hook that pulled a product ID from a data-attribute — in QA the IDs matched the expected values so the hook returned the correct slug and everything passed. In production the IDs were different, so the hook fed the wrong value, the slug resolved incorrectly, and the button routed to a 404. Classic environment parity gap — it was invisible in QA because QA data matched the assumption baked into the code. Once I identified the mismatch I wrote a targeted PR that passed the correct production ID through the data-attribute, kept the scope tight to avoid touching anything else, and dropped it in the channel when it was ready.
- **Result:** Our lead picked up my PR and used it to fix the issue on the spot. The release went out within the window. The lesson: under pressure the worst thing you can do is wait for someone else to finish. You read the room, find the lane that isn't covered, and move into it quietly. Collaboration under pressure isn't about who solves it — it's about making sure it gets solved.

