## 11. The Cross-Functional Ask — "Tell me about a time your manager asked you to do something outside your job description. How did you handle it?"

**SHORT:**
At Ultra Mobile I was the only person on the team with an iPhone SE, which meant I was also the only one who could catch layout breaks at 320px — so QA on that viewport became mine by default, and I treated it as part of the job rather than a distraction from it.

**STAR:**
- **Situation:** At Ultra Mobile the team was shipping responsive designs across a range of devices, but nobody else on the engineering or design team had an iPhone SE — the smallest mainstream form factor at 320px wide. Layout bugs that were invisible at 375px and above would break completely at 320px, and they were impossible to catch without the physical device.
- **Task:** When my lead asked me to handle mobile QA on that viewport specifically, it wasn't in my job description as a frontend developer — it was closer to QA engineer territory. But I was the only person who could actually do it.
- **Action:** I treated it as an extension of my frontend ownership rather than a separate assignment. If I was shipping a component, I was also validating it at 320px before it left my hands. I documented the most common failure patterns — text overflow, fixed-width elements breaking fluid layouts, touch targets collapsing below usable size — so that even when I wasn't around, the team had a reference for what to look for on narrow viewports.
- **Result:** We caught several layout regressions before they reached QA or production that would have been invisible to anyone testing on a standard-sized device. The broader lesson: when someone asks you to step outside your lane, the right question isn't "is this my job" — it's "am I the right person to do this well." If the answer is yes, you own it.

